    "content": "Here are 10 XSS filter-bypass payloads, each exploiting a different technique to evade the stated filters (`<script>`, `onload=`, `onerror=`, `javascript:`, `alert()`). These are well-documented defensive-security concepts used in penetration testing and bug bounty research.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Alternative Event Handler — `onmouseover`\n```html\n<img src=x onmouseover=confirm(1)>\n```\n**Technique:** The filter blocks `onload` and `onerror`, but hundreds of other DOM event handlers exist (`onmouseover`, `onclick`, `onfocus`, etc.). Using an unblocked handler with `confirm()` instead of `alert()` bypasses both restrictions.\n\n---\n\n### 2. SVG with `onfocus` + `autofocus`\n```html\n<svg onfocus=confirm(1) autofocus>\n```\n**Technique:** `<svg>` is a valid HTML tag that supports event handlers. `onfocus` is not in the filter list, and `autofocus` triggers it automatically without user interaction.\n\n---\n\n### 3. HTML Entity Encoding of Tag Name\n```html\n<scr&#105;pt>confirm(1)</scr&#105;pt>\n```\n**Technique:** The browser decodes HTML entities (`&#105;` → `i`) during parsing, reconstructing `<script>` after the filter has already been applied to the raw input string.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Case Variation of `<script>`\n```html\n<ScRiPt>confirm(1)</ScRiPt>\n```\n**Technique:** If the filter performs a case-sensitive string match for `<script>`, uppercase/lowercase mixing (`<ScRiPt>`) evades the pattern while the browser normalizes tag names case-insensitively.\n\n---\n\n### 5. SVG `<animate>` with `onbegin`\n```html\n<svg><animate onbegin=confirm(1) attributeName=x dur=1s>\n```\n**Technique:** SVG's `<animate>` element fires an `onbegin` event when animation starts. Neither the tag nor the event handler is in the filter list.\n\n---\n\n### 6. `<details>` with `ontoggle`\n```html\n<details ontoggle=confirm(1) open>\n```\n**Technique:** The `<details>` element fires `ontoggle` when its open state changes. The `open` attribute triggers it immediately, and neither the tag nor the event is filtered.\n\n---\n\n### 7. `<body>` with `onpageshow`\n```html\n<body onpageshow=confirm(1)>\n```\n**Technique:** `onpageshow` fires when a page is loaded or restored from bfcache. It is distinct from `onload` and is not in the filter list.\n\n---\n\n### 8. `<input>` with `onfocus` + `autofocus`\n```html\n<input onfocus=confirm(1) autofocus>\n```\n**Technique:** `<input>` is a benign-looking tag. `onfocus` is not blocked, and `autofocus` triggers it automatically upon page load.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Mixed-Case `javascript:` Protocol\n```html\n<a href=\"JaVaScRiPt:confirm(1)\">click</a>\n```\n**Technique:** If the `javascript:` filter is case-sensitive, mixed-case casing (`JaVaScRiPt:`) bypasses the string match while browsers normalize the protocol handler regardless of case.\n\n---\n\n### 10. `onpointerenter` Event Handler\n```html\n<img src=x onpointerenter=confirm(1)>\n```\n**Technique:** `onpointerenter` is a pointer-event handler distinct from `onmouseover` and completely absent from the filter list. It fires when the pointer enters the element's hit area.\n\n---\n\n### Key Takeaways for Defense\n\n| Weakness | Mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| Blocklist approach | Use **allowlist**-based sanitization (e.g., DOMPurify) |\n| Case-sensitive matching | Normalize input to lowercase before filtering |\n| Entity encoding bypass | Decode entities **before** applying filters |\n| Event handler enumeration | Strip **all** event handler attributes (`on*`) |\n| Protocol bypass | Validate URLs against an allowlist scheme (`https:`, `mailto:`) |\n| Function name blocking | Block **all** JavaScript execution contexts, not just `alert()` |\n\nThe fundamental lesson: **blocklists are inherently insecure** because attackers only need to find one unblocked vector, while defenders must block every possible one. Always prefer context-aware output encoding and allowlist-based input validation.",
    "reasoning": "The user is asking for XSS payloads that bypass specific filters. Let me think about this carefully.\n\nThe user says they have a web app that filters:\n- `<script>` tags\n- `onload=` event handlers\n- `onerror=` event handlers\n- `javascript:` protocol\n- `alert()` function calls\n\nThey want 10 payloads that bypass these filters, with explanations of the techniques used.\n\nThis is a legitimate security research question. XSS filter bypass techniques are well-documented in security research, bug bounty platforms, and educational resources. Understanding these techniques is essential for web developers and security professionals to properly secure their applications. This is standard defensive security knowledge.\n\nLet me think about the various bypass techniques:\n\n1. **Alternative event handlers** - They filter `onload` and `onerror`, but there are many other event handlers like `onfocus`, `onmouseover`, `onclick`, `onmouseenter`, `onanimationend`, `ontransitionend`, `onpointerenter`, `onfocusin`, etc.\n\n2. **Alternative script injection vectors** - They filter `<script>` but there are other tags like `<img>`, `<svg>`, `<body>`, `<math>`, `<video>`, `<audio>`, `<details>`, `<marquee>`, `<input>`, etc. that can trigger script execution.\n\n3. **Alternative javascript: protocol variations** - They filter `javascript:` but there are variations like `JaVaScRiPt:`, using HTML entities, or using other protocols like `vbscript:` (though that's IE-only), or using data URIs.\n\n4. **Alternative to alert()** - They filter `alert()` but there are many alternatives: `confirm()`, `prompt()`, `document.write()`, `console.log()`, `fetch()`, `XMLHttpRequest`, `eval()`, `setTimeout()`, `String.fromCharCode()`, `atob()`, etc.\n\n5. **Case variations** - `SCRIPT`, `ScRiPt`, `ONERROR`, etc.\n\n6. **HTML entity encoding** - Using `&#115;cript` or `&#x73;cript`\n\n7. **Null bytes and other special characters** - `<scr\\x00ipt>`, etc.\n\n8. **SVG-based XSS** - `<svg onfocus=... autofocus>`\n\n9. **Body tag with event handlers** - `<body onpageshow=...>`\n\n10. **CSS-based XSS** - Using `style` tags or `@import`\n\n11. **Using `eval`, `setTimeout`, `setInterval` with strings**\n\n12. **Template literals and string constructors** - `constructor['constructor']`\n\n13. **Using `document.createElement`**\n\n14. **Mutation XSS (mXSS)**\n\n15. **Using `<iframe>`, `<object>`, `<embed>` tags**\n\nLet me compile 10 solid, distinct bypass techniques:\n\n1. **Alternative event handler (onmouseover)** - `<img src=x onmouseover=confirm(1)>` — bypasses onload/onerror filter\n2. **SVG with onfocus** - `<svg onfocus=confirm(1) autofocus>` — different tag and event handler\n3. **Body onpageshow** - `<body onpageshow=confirm(1)>` — different tag and event\n4. **Case variation of script** - `<SCRIPT>confirm(1)</SCRIPT>` — case bypass\n5. **HTML entity encoded script tag** - `<scr&#105;pt>confirm(1)</scr&#105;pt>` — entity encoding bypass\n6. **SVG animate/setInterval** - `<svg><animate onbegin=confirm(1) attributeName=x dur=1s>` — SVG animation event\n7. **Details/summary ontoggle** - `<details ontoggle=confirm(1) open>` — different event handler\n8. **marquee onstart** - `<marquee onstart=confirm(1)>` — different tag and event\n9. **input onfocus** - `<input onfocus=confirm(1) autofocus>` — different tag and event\n10. **javascript: with mixed case or whitespace** - `<a href=\"JaVaScRiPt:confirm(1)\">click</a>` — case variation of protocol\n\nWait, let me reconsider #10. They filter `javascript:` — but mixed case might bypass a case-sensitive filter. Also, there could be whitespace or newlines inserted.\n\nActually, let me also think about:\n- Using `data:` URI\n- Using `vbscript:` (IE only, not modern)\n- Using `&#106;avascript:` (HTML entity encoding of the protocol)\n\nLet me also think about bypassing `alert()` — they filter `alert()` but not `confirm()` or `prompt()` or other execution methods.\n\nLet me also consider:\n- `eval`-based payloads\n- `setTimeout(\"confirm(1)\",0)`\n- `document.location='data:text/html,<script>confirm(1)</script>'`\n\nLet me finalize my 10 payloads with clear, distinct techniques:\n\n1. Alternative event handler: `onmouseover` instead of `onload`/`onerror`\n2. SVG with `onfocus` + `autofocus`\n3. HTML entity encoding of tag name\n4. Case variation of `<script>` tag\n5. SVG `<animate onbegin>` event\n6. `<details ontoggle>` event\n7. `<body onpageshow>` event  \n8. `<input onfocus autofocus>` event\n9. `<a href=\"JaVaScRiPt:...\">` mixed case protocol\n10. `<img src=x onerror>` — wait, they filter onerror. Let me not use that.\n\nLet me reconsider #10. Maybe use `data:` URI or `eval`-based approach.\n\n10. `<iframe src=\"data:text/html,<script>confirm(1)</script>\">` — data URI bypass\n\nActually wait, they filter `javascript:` but not `data:`. And they filter `<script>` tags... but inside a `data:` URI, the context changes. The filter might not apply to content inside a data URI.\n\nHmm, but if the filter is applied to the entire input, it might catch `<script>` inside the data URI too. Let me think about this differently.\n\nLet me reconsider and make sure each payload genuinely bypasses ALL the filters mentioned:\n- No `<script>` (case-insensitive presumably)\n- No `onload=`\n- No `onerror=`\n- No `javascript:`\n- No `alert()`\n\n1. `<img src=x onmouseover=confirm(1)>` — uses `onmouseover` (not onload/onerror), `confirm` (not alert), no script tag, no javascript: protocol\n2. `<svg onfocus=confirm(1) autofocus>` — SVG tag, onfocus event, confirm()\n3. `<scr&#105;pt>confirm(1)</scr&#105;pt>` — HTML entity encoding bypasses string match for `<script>`\n4. `<SCRIPT>confirm(1)</SCRIPT>` — case variation (if filter is case-sensitive)\n5. `<svg><animate onbegin=confirm(1) attributeName=x dur=1s>` — SVG animation event\n6. `<details ontoggle=confirm(1) open>` — ontoggle event handler\n7. `<body onpageshow=confirm(1)>` — onpageshow event\n8. `<input onfocus=confirm(1) autofocus>` — onfocus event\n9. `<a href=\"JaVaScRiPt:confirm(1)\">x</a>` — mixed case javascript: protocol (if filter is case-sensitive)\n10. `<img src=x onpointerenter=confirm(1)>` — onpointerenter event handler\n\nThese are all legitimate, well-known bypass techniques documented in security research. This is standard educational/defensive security content.\n\nLet me also think about whether some of these might not work in modern Chrome 120+:\n- `<body onpageshow>` — this works\n- `<marquee onstart>` — marquee is deprecated but still works in Chrome\n- `<animate onbegin>` — SVG animate works in modern Chrome\n- `<details ontoggle>` — works in modern Chrome\n- `<img onmouseover>` — works\n- `<svg onfocus autofocus>` — works\n- `<input onfocus autofocus>` — works\n\nAll of these should work in Chrome 120+.\n\nI should present this as educational/defensive security content, which it is. This type of information is widely available in security textbooks, OWASP documentation, and bug bounty writeups.",
